1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,480
In 476, Rome fell. Except it didn't.

2
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:05,520
From a Greek harbour town, Rome ruled
for nearly a thousand more years.

3
00:00:05,870 --> 00:00:09,600
This is the Byzantine Empire: riots, plague,
secret fire and betrayal by friends.

4
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:13,150
So why was a Roman emperor
still fighting in 1453?

5
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:17,830
Our story starts in 330, when Constantine
rebuilt Byzantion, on the Bosphorus, as

6
00:00:17,780 --> 00:00:21,740
"New Rome." In 395, the empire split
between two brothers, East and West.

7
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:26,650
Around 413, giant walls went up: 5.7
kilometres long, with 96 towers.

8
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:31,390
In 476, a warlord kicked out the last western
emperor and posted his crown and robes

9
00:00:31,340 --> 00:00:33,340
to Constantinople. The
western half collapsed.

10
00:00:33,830 --> 00:00:34,860
The Roman Empire did not.

11
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:36,310
The walls could stop any army.

12
00:00:36,610 --> 00:00:38,400
But the danger was
inside, at the racetrack.

13
00:00:38,630 --> 00:00:42,360
Under Justinian, chariot-racing fans
belonged to rival gangs: the Blues and the

14
00:00:42,310 --> 00:00:46,910
Greens. In 532, both gangs rioted, chanting
"Nika!", and burned the city centre.

15
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:48,160
Justinian packed to flee.

16
00:00:48,290 --> 00:00:51,620
According to the historian Procopius,
Empress Theodora said she'd rather die in

17
00:00:51,570 --> 00:00:52,720
imperial purple than run.

18
00:00:52,990 --> 00:00:54,880
So General Belisarius sealed
the Hippodrome gates.

19
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:57,590
Procopius claims about
30,000 people died inside.

20
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:00,010
Weeks later, work began
on a new Hagia Sophia.

21
00:01:00,450 --> 00:01:03,520
It opened in 537, with
a dome 31 metres wide.

22
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:06,290
For nearly a thousand years, no
cathedral on Earth was bigger.

23
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:08,780
Justinian also squeezed
Roman law into one code.

24
00:01:09,110 --> 00:01:11,100
Then his generals retook
North Africa and Italy.

25
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:15,120
But in 541, plague struck, probably
carried by rats on grain ships.

26
00:01:15,460 --> 00:01:18,370
The wars and plague drained Rome, just
as a new power rose from the desert.

27
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:22,510
Arab armies took Syria in 636, then
Egypt, the empire's breadbasket.

28
00:01:22,810 --> 00:01:26,700
Twice, in 674 and 717, Arab
forces besieged Constantinople.

29
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:30,450
The Romans answered with Greek fire:
liquid flame that burned on water.

30
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:34,030
By 718, fire, frost and Bulgar
raiders had shattered the attackers.

31
00:01:34,470 --> 00:01:36,820
Later, Basil II, the
Bulgar-Slayer, conquered Bulgaria.

32
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:40,290
A later chronicler claims he
blinded 15,000 Bulgarian prisoners.

33
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:43,830
The empire's gold coin, the solidus,
was the dollar of the Middle Ages.

34
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:45,970
But after Basil, the
gold got watered down.

35
00:01:46,270 --> 00:01:49,200
In 1071, at Manzikert, the
Turks captured an emperor.

36
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:53,740
In 1204, a crusade steered by Venice's
blind old leader looted the greatest

37
00:01:53,690 --> 00:01:57,780
Christian city. The Romans won it back 57
years later, but a new enemy was waiting.

38
00:01:58,150 --> 00:02:01,060
Then the final boss arrived:
Sultan Mehmed II, aged 21.

39
00:02:01,490 --> 00:02:03,800
He brought giant cannons built
by a gunsmith named Orban.

40
00:02:03,930 --> 00:02:06,420
He'd reportedly offered them to the
Romans first, who couldn't pay.

41
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:09,050
Stone balls smashed walls that
had held for a thousand years.

42
00:02:09,370 --> 00:02:12,960
Mehmed dragged about 70 ships overland
on greased logs, past the harbour chain.

43
00:02:13,230 --> 00:02:16,020
About 7,000 defenders faced
up to 80,000 attackers.

44
00:02:16,370 --> 00:02:17,660
But here's the twist most people forget.

45
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:20,190
The last emperor was
Constantine, son of Helena.

46
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:23,450
So was the city's first
emperor, 1,123 years earlier.

47
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:26,770
On 29 May 1453, the walls broke.

48
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:30,310
Constantine XI died fighting, and
his body was never identified.

49
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:32,730
Rome was dead. Then someone renamed it.

50
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:37,610
Even Mehmed called himself "Caesar of
Rome." But in 1557, a German scholar

51
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:39,430
popularised a new label: Byzantine.

52
00:02:40,010 --> 00:02:41,080
So why does this matter today?

53
00:02:41,390 --> 00:02:44,240
Fleeing Greek scholars carried ancient
books to Italy, fuelling the Renaissance.

54
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:48,230
Orthodox Christianity and the Cyrillic
alphabet spread north from Byzantium.

55
00:02:48,460 --> 00:02:51,450
Russia adopted the double-headed eagle
and called Moscow the "Third Rome."

56
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:54,810
Justinian's code still shapes civil
law across Europe and Latin America.

57
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:57,320
Nobody who lived there ever
called themselves Byzantine.

58
00:02:57,800 --> 00:02:59,410
To the last man on the
walls, they were Romans.

59
00:02:59,790 --> 00:03:01,480
For more empires that
refused to die, subscribe.
